  "normative_ethical_theory_explanation": "Virtue ethics focuses on the character of the moral agent and what a practically wise, good person would do, rather than on rigid rules or pure outcome calculations. It emphasizes cultivating virtues—compassion, courage, justice, temperance—and exercising context-sensitive judgment (phronesis). In tragic dilemmas where every option involves wrongdoing or harm, a virtuous person aims to honor the core virtues and choose the lesser harm while accepting a ‘moral remainder’—appropriate regret and responsibility. Thus, the action is justified not as mere numbers but as an expression of good character responding wisely and humanely to an unavoidable tragedy."
}

